    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> held roughly three fifths of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s territory and population and became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary democracy</a> under its constitution of 30 November 1920.</li><li>Under Minister President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-braun">Otto Braun</a> and Interior Minister Carl Severing it was considered a bulwark of democracy within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-von-papen">Franz von Papen</a>'s Prussian coup d'état of July 1932 removed its government; under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-goring">Hermann Göring</a> it served the Nazi regime until the Allied Control Council abolished it on 25 February 1947.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gestapo">Gestapo</a> grew out of the Prussian political police, which came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-goring">Hermann Göring</a> after Hitler named him Reich commissioner of the Interior for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1933.</p></aside>
    </section>

