    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The German revolution, begun by the sailors' mutiny at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiel">Kiel</a>, reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> on 9 November 1918, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-maximilian-of-baden">Max von Baden</a> announced the abdication of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a> as Emperor and King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-ebert">Friedrich Ebert</a> charged Paul Hirsch of the MSPD with keeping order in Prussia, and on 12 November councils' representatives declared the old government deposed, promising to make Prussia a fully democratic part of the unified republic. A revolutionary cabinet of the MSPD and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/independent-social-democratic-party-of-germany">USPD</a>, chaired jointly by Hirsch and Heinrich Ströbel, confiscated the royal property and abolished the House of Lords on 15 November.</p><p>Prussia's survival was not assured: the Centre Party's council in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine-province">Rhine Province</a> called for a Rhineland-Westphalian republic, 600,000 signed for autonomy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, and a revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Posen</a> aimed at restoring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Hugo Preuss envisaged breaking Prussia up. The State Assembly, elected on 26 January 1919 by universal suffrage that replaced the three-class franchise, resolved against a breakup, and a Weimar Coalition of the MSPD, Centre and DDP governed from March 1919.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> had roughly three fifths of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s territory and population; 61% of Germany's people lived there in 1925. Its density, 130.7 inhabitants per square kilometre, hid large contrasts: 60.9 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a> against 295.6 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine-province">Rhine Province</a>. In 1925 industry and the skilled trades employed 41.3% of workers, agriculture 22% and trade and transport 17.5%, but in East Prussia agriculture employed 45.4%, and the agrarian east remained distinct from the industrial west.</p><p>Of those employed in 1925, 46.8% were blue-collar workers, 17.1% salaried employees and civil servants and 16.2% self-employed. In 1928 average income in Berlin-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> was 1,566 Reichsmarks, more than 30% above the national average, while East Prussia's was 814. Greater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> was formed in 1920 by incorporating 8 cities and 59 rural communities. The Braun government abolished the 12,000 manorial districts, home to 1.5 million people, in 1927, though in 1928 83% of an average East Prussian farm worker's income still came as wages in kind. The pupil-teacher ratio fell from 55 in 1911 to 38 in 1928.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> lost its army with the transfer of military responsibilities to the national government, and there was no Prussian Minister of War after 1919. Its force was the police, the strongest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the government's chief instrument for keeping constitutional order. Early violence was heavy: fighting with the People's Navy Division in December 1918 was followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartacist-uprising">Spartacist uprising</a> of 5–12 January 1919, with an estimated 150 to 200 dead, strikes of 350,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a> miners and the first Silesian uprising of August 1919, put down by military means.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapp-putsch">Kapp Putsch</a> of March 1920, backed chiefly by the large landowners of East Elbia, Severing and the police chief Wilhelm Abegg made the force loyal to the Republic; by 1928 fifteen of thirty police chiefs were Social Democrats, though the rank and file remained largely conservative. Prussia banned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> in 1922, the Red Front Fighters after Blood May in 1929 and the SA in 1931. After 1933 the police under Göring became an important element in enforcing Nazi rule.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The constitution adopted on 30 November 1920, by 280 votes to 60, gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> no state president. The Landtag, elected for four years, chose the minister president, who had policy-making authority and appointed the other ministers. A State Council elected by the provincial parliaments, chaired until 1933 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-adenauer">Konrad Adenauer</a>, could initiate laws and object to them. There were twelve provinces plus <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, each with a governor (Oberpräsident) and a parliament, and 361 rural and 116 urban districts.</p><p>With brief interruptions the SPD held the premiership: Hirsch, Adam Stegerwald in 1921, a grand coalition under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-braun">Otto Braun</a> from November 1921 to 1925, Wilhelm <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a> briefly, and Braun again from 3 April 1925. The "Braun-Severing system" purged the administration of republic-hostile officials: about a hundred senior civil servants were retired, and by 1929 291 of 540 political officials belonged to the coalition parties. The judiciary resisted reform; one estimate of 1932 held that only about 5% of judges were republican-minded.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> was more politically stable than the Republic itself. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-stresemann">Gustav Stresemann</a> called the Prussia of 1923 the "bulwark of German republicans", and its grand coalition helped the Republic survive that crisis year. The SPD's readiness to govern in Prussia, which it lacked nationally in 1920, 1923 and 1930, was one reason; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-braun">Otto Braun</a> claimed that Prussia had never been governed in a more Prussian manner than during his term. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-goebbels">Joseph Goebbels</a> wrote in 1930 that whoever had Prussia also had the Reich.</p><p>The Free State also reshaped religion and education. The Prussian Concordat with the Vatican, signed on 14 June 1929 by Eugenio Pacelli, ended the last church laws of the Kulturkampf and re-established the bishoprics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, while a treaty with the Protestant churches followed in 1931. Its territory, however, bore most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s losses under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>: Eupen-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malmedy">Malmedy</a>, the Hultschiner Ländchen, Danzig, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Memel</a> Territory and much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Posen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-prussia">West Prussia</a>, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a> cut off by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-corridor">Polish Corridor</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the Landtag election of 1932 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">NSDAP</a> rose from 1.8% to 36.7% and, with the KPD, held a negative majority; Braun's caretaker government stayed in office. After 18 people died in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altona">Altona</a> Bloody Sunday on 17 July, Hindenburg used Article 48 to dismiss the Prussian ministers and appoint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-von-papen">Franz von Papen</a> Reich commissioner. On 25 October 1932 the Reich Constitutional Court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> found the removal illegal, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> was left with two governments, one without power.</p><p>After Hitler became chancellor, Göring took over the Prussian Interior, the Landtag was dissolved and, after the March 1933 election, confirmed the dismissal of the Braun government. The coordination laws of 31 March and 7 April subordinated Prussia to the Reich, Göring became minister president on 11 April, and the Landtag met for the last time on 18 May 1933, the SPD alone refusing the enabling act. The laws of 1934 and 1935 dissolved the states in fact. In 1945 the lands east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oder-neisse-line">Oder–Neisse line</a> went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and an estimated twelve million Germans fled or were expelled; Control Council Law No. 46 of 25 February 1947 dissolved Prussia, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> citing its history of militarism.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Free State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> was the dominant state of Weimar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, a democracy led mostly by the SPD's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-braun">Otto Braun</a> and Carl Severing that reformed its civil service and police and was called a bulwark of the Republic. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prussian-coup-of-1932">Prussian coup of 1932</a> and the Nazi seizure of power hollowed it out, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> abolished it in 1947.</em></p>

